pancake ships a set of composable C ABI archives and four ways to hold them from
another language. Together the bindings are one of the three consumption
[surfaces]($link.page('surfaces')).
Each binding mirrors the archive set: a core module over `libpancake_ocapn`, plus
one per-transport module that loads or links only its own archive. You add
transports à la carte, exactly as a C consumer links archives or a Zig consumer
imports modules.

Every module presents an idiomatic API on top, so what you write is Rust,
TypeScript, Python, or Racket rather than foreign function calls. Each binding
has its own dogfood build step (`rust-dogfood`, `deno-dogfood`, and their
siblings) that drives a real OCapN round-trip, so wire-shape or ABI regressions
surface where they would in production.

[All four bindings wrap the same C ABI. The shared surface is what lets an app read the same way in every language.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-bindings.svg'))

## One surface, four idioms

An application holds one object, a `Connection`, and uses the small set of
application verbs the
[bindings contract](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/README.md)
defines. The contract is the authoritative description of the
surface and the value model beneath it; this section is the per-language tour.

The shape is uniform on purpose. You fetch a [capability]($link.page('concepts')) by
name, spawn a local object, and hand a partner a reference to it; the partner
calls it back. There is no export-position bookkeeping, no manual flush, and no
hand-rolled receive loop. A capability is a `Ref`, and a `Ref` marshals itself to
the right wire reference when you pass it as an argument. That one move is why
delegation, attenuation, and revocation are ordinary `spawn` and `send` code
instead of special APIs.

> **Ref** is the binding's word for a capability in hand: a live reference to an
> object, yours or a partner's. Holding one is the right to call that object, and
> the only way to get one is to be given it. Passing a `Ref` as an argument is how
> that authority moves between peers.

| Binding | Entry point | Async idiom | Actor idiom |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| [Rust]($link.page('bindings/rust')) | `connect`, `Connection`, typed enums | tokio (opt-in `async`) | `spawn` over closures |
| [TypeScript]($link.page('bindings/ts')) | `connect`, `Connection` class | native promises | `spawn` over callbacks |
| [Python]($link.page('bindings/python')) | `connect`, `Connection`, native values | `asyncio` | `spawn` over callables |
| [Racket]($link.page('bindings/racket')) | `connect`, `connection`, event structs | green threads + ports | Goblins-faithful |

Every binding enters the same way: `connect` takes a locator and returns the
`Connection` described above, and all four ride the shared byte-fed host in
the library — one runtime, four idioms over it.

## Sync, async, and the actor layer

Each binding gives you levels, and you stay at the one your app needs. The
async connection runs over the language's own non-blocking sockets, with no
thread per call and no blocking netlayer. The actor layer is the Goblins-shaped
top, where an actor is a plain native callable and an eventual-send to a
partner-hosted reference travels over the wire and settles a promise. Racket's
layer is the reference shape, because it reads the way a
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) programmer expects.

> **Vat**: a single-threaded home for local objects. Each call runs as one *turn*
> before the next begins. The [modules page]($link.page('modules')) draws the full picture.

## Encryption rides a separate artifact

Cleartext TCP ships in the pure-Zig archives. Encrypted transport is
[iroh](https://www.iroh.computer/) QUIC through a separate optional library,
`libpancake_net_transport_iroh`, with per-binding modules that load only for an
`iroh` locator. See [Transports]($link.page('transports')) for how hosting and
dialing differ from TCP.

## Getting the archives

No prebuilt archives ship yet. Every binding opens the shared pancake archives,
so build them with `zig build` in a pancake checkout; a Zig toolchain is the one
prerequisite. Each binding finds them the same way: an explicit
`PANCAKE_LIB_DIR` wins, a binding running in-tree falls back to
the checkout's `zig-out/lib`, and otherwise the OS dynamic-linker search resolves
the plain library name from the system library path.

## Run the dogfoods

The dogfoods are the repository's own verification gates, run from a checkout
rather than a consumer install:

```sh
zig build bindings        # rust + deno + bun + python + racket dogfoods
zig build rust-dogfood    # just the Rust consumer
```

These need `cargo`, `deno`, `bun`, `python3`, and `racket` on PATH. The
[devcontainer](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/.devcontainer/)
provides all five.
